atal development generates two distinct anxiogenic and neuroendocrine profiles in adulthood</article-title><source>Endocrinology</source><year>2006</year><volume>147</volume><fpage>2506</fpage><lpage>2517</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16455779</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1210/en.2005-1054</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B5"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Pallares</surname><given-names>ME</given-names></name><name><surname>Scacchi Bernasconi</surname><given-names>PA</given-names></name><name><surname>Feleder</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Cutrera</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Effects of prenatal stress on motor performance and anxiety behavior in Swiss mice</article-title><source>Physiol Behav</source><year>2007</year><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17822726</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B6"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mueller</surname><given-names>BR</given-names></name><name><surname>Bale</surname><given-names>TL</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Early prenatal stress impact on coping strategies and learning performance is sex dependent</article-title><source>Physiol Behav</source><year>2007</year><volume>91</volume><fpage>55</fpage><lpage>65</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17367828</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.physbeh.2007.01.017</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B7"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Talge</surname><given-names>NM</given-names></name><name><surname>Neal</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Glover</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Antenatal maternal stress and long-term effects on child neurodevelopment: how and why?</article-title><source>Journal of child psychology and psychiatry, and allied di